### Step 1: Add Same Suit Binary Feature
python
# Feature name: same_suit
# Feature description: Binary indicator (0 or 1) that checks if all five cards share the same suit.
# Usefulness: This feature simplifies the detection of Flush, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush hands, all of which require all cards to have the same suit. It reduces the complexity of the downstream model when identifying these hands.

df['same_suit'] = (df[['V1', 'V3', 'V5', 'V7', 'V9']].nunique(axis=1) == 1).astype(int)
end

---

### Step 2: Add Consecutive Rank Feature
python
# Feature name: rank_is_consecutive
# Feature description: Binary indicator (0 or 1) that checks if the ranks of the five cards are sequential (forming a straight).
# Usefulness: Consecutive rank sequences are critical for identifying Straight, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush hands. This feature captures whether the ranks form an uninterrupted sequence.

df['rank_is_consecutive'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(
    lambda row: sorted(row) == list(range(min(row), max(row) + 1)), axis=1).astype(int)
end

---

### Step 3: Add Maximum Rank Frequency Feature
python
# Feature name: max_rank_frequency
# Feature description: Calculates the maximum frequency of any rank in the hand (i.e., the number of times the most common rank appears).
# Usefulness: Poker hands like One Pair, Two Pairs, Three of a Kind, Full House, and Four of a Kind depend on how many cards share the same rank. This feature directly quantifies the frequency of the most common rank, which is highly predictive for these hands.

df['max_rank_frequency'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(
    lambda row: max([row.count(rank) for rank in set(row)]), axis=1)
end

---

