ncies.\ndf['rank_count_mode'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(lambda row: max([row.count(rank) for rank in set(row)]), axis=1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Columns Dropped Explanation\nDrop any redundant columns that originate as parts of other engineered features but do not contribute new meaningful information independently. Since the problem provided does not specify such columns that can be immediately removed, no columns are dropped in these steps.", "### Possible next steps to improve predictions through feature engineering:\n\n#### Step 1: Add a Binary Feature for Same Suit Cards\npython\n# Feature name\ndf['same_suit'] = ((df['V1'] == df['V3']) & (df['V3'] == df['V5']) & (df['V5'] == df['V7']) & (df['V7'] == df['V9'])).astype(int)\n\n# Feature description\n# Binary indicator (0 or 1) that checks if all five cards have the same suit.\n\n# Usefulness:\n# This directly contributes to the detection of Flush and Straight Flush hands (Target categories 5 and 8),\n# which require all cards to have the same suit. It simplifies downstream model learning for these poker hands.\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Add a Rank Sequence Check for Straight Detection\npython\n# Feature name\ndf['is_straight'] = (\n    (\n        ((df['V2'] + 4 == df['V4'] + 3 == df['V6'] + 2 == df['V8'] + 1 == df['V10']) |\n         (df['V2'] + 4 == df['V4'] + 3 == df['V6'] + 2 == df['V8'] + 1 == df['V10'])  &\n        )\n    ).to_inter ger\nsimilar!)", "Here is the best possible solution with three feature-engineering steps that aim to intuitively improve the downstream model's predictive performance. \n\n### Step 1: Add Same Suit Binary Feature\npython\n# Feature name: same_suit\n# Feature description: Binary indicator (0 or 1) that checks if all five cards share the same suit.\n# Usefulness: This feature simplifies the detection of Flush, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush hands, all of which require all cards to have the same suit. It reduces the complexity of the downstream model when identifying these hands.\n\ndf['same_suit'] = (df[['V1', 'V3', 'V5', 'V7', 'V9']].nunique(axis=1) == 1).astype(int)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Add Consecutive Rank Feature\npython\n# Feature name: rank_is_consecutive\n# Feature description: Binary indicator (0 or 1) that checks if the ranks of the five cards are sequential (forming a straight).\n# Usefulness: Consecutive rank sequences are critical for identifying Straight, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush hands. This feature captures whether the ranks form an uninterrupted sequence.\n\ndf['rank_is_consecutive'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(\n    lambda row: sorted(row) == list(range(min(row), max(row) + 1)), axis=1).astype(int)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add Maximum Rank Frequency Feature\npython\n# Feature name: max_rank_frequency\n# Feature description: Calculates the maximum frequency of any rank in the hand (i.e., the number of times the most common rank appears).\n# Usefulness: Poker hands like One Pair, Two Pairs, Three of a Kind, Full House, and Four of a Kind depend on how many cards share the same rank. This feature directly quantifies the frequency of the most common rank, which is highly predictive for these hands.\n\ndf['max_rank_frequency'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(\n    lambda row: max([row.count(rank) for rank in set(row)]), axis=1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Explanation for Dropping Columns (If Needed)\nCurrently, the features derived above are not redundant and do not justify dropping any columns, as all the engineered features provide unique and useful information for predicting poker hands. \n\n--- \n\nBy combining the features: same_suit, rank_is_consecutive, and max_rank_frequency, the model is likely to capture patterns relating to Flushes, Straights, Pairs, Three/Four of a Kind, and Full Houses, significantly improving its prediction performance.", "Here are three simple, direct, and intuitive feature engineering steps that can improve poker hand prediction while adhering to the dataset's description and attributes. Each step explains the reasoning behind the engineered feature and ensures there are no redundant or overly complex calculations.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Add a Binary Feature for Identifying a Flush Hand\npython\n# Feature name: is_flush\n# Feature description: Determines if all five cards have the same suit.\n# Usefulness: Flush, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush hands require all five cards to have the same suit. This binary feature simplifies the model's ability to identify these hands.\n\ndf['is_flush'] = ((df['V1'] == df['V3']) & (df['V3'] == df['V5']) & (df['V5'] == df['V7']) & (df['V7'] == df['V9'])).astype(int)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Add a Binary Feature for Identifying a Straight Hand\npython\n# Feature name: is_straight\n# Feature description: Checks if the card ranks form a consecutive sequence with no gaps.\n# Usefulness: Hands like Straight, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush require ranks to be in a consecutive sequence. This feature enables the model to identify such patterns efficiently.\n\ndf['is_straight'] = (\n    df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']]\n    .apply(lambda row: sorted(row) == list(range(min(row), max(row) + 1)), axis=1)\n).astype(int)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add the Maximum Rank Count for a Hand\npython\n# Feature name: max_rank_count\n# Feature description: Calculates the maximum count of any rank in the hand (e.g., 2 for a pair, 4 for Four of a Kind).\n# Usefulness: Most poker hands (e.g., One Pair, Two Pairs, Three of a Kind, Full House, and Four of a Kind) are defined by how many cards have the same rank. This feature efficiently captures that information.\n\ndf['max_rank_count'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(lambda row: max([row.count(rank) for rank in set(row)]), axis=1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Explanation for Dropping Redundant Columns (if applicable)\nThese features do not explicitly drop any columns but make other existing features (like using all suit columns to detect Flush) less critical when the new features (is_flush, is_straight, etc.) are included. If deemed redundant in future iterations, suit and rank-specific columns might be dropped as derived features progressively outperform them.", "### Solution: Three Feature Engineering Steps for Poker Hand Prediction\n\nHere are three intuitive and direct feature engineering steps to improve the downstream model's performance while avoiding previously rejected solutions. Each step is carefully aligned with the dataset's attributes and the poker hand descriptions.\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: is_flush\n# Feature description: Binary indicator (0 or 1) denoting whether all cards in the hand share the same suit.\n# Usefulness: Poker hands like Flush, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush require all five cards to have the same suit. This feature simplifies the detection of these hands for the model.\n\ndf['is_flush'] = (df[['V1', 'V3', 'V5', 'V7', 'V9']].nunique(axis=1) == 1).astype(int)\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: is_straight\n# Feature description: Binary indicator (0 or 1) that determines whether the ranks of the five cards are sequentially ordered with no gaps.\n# Usefulness: Sequentially ordered ranks are critical for identifying poker hands like Straight, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush, which depend on consecutive card ranks.\n\ndf['is_straight'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(\n    lambda row: sorted(row) == list(range(min(row), max(row) + 1)), axis=1).astype(int)\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Feature name: max_rank_count\n# Feature description: Calculates the maximum count of any rank in the hand (e.g., 2 for One Pair, 3 for Three of a Kind, 4 for Four of a Kind).\n# Usefulness: Most poker hands (e.g., One Pair, Two Pairs, Three of a Kind, Full House, and Four of a Kind) are defined by the repetition of ranks. Capturing the maximum frequency of any rank directly helps the model identify these hands.\n\ndf['max_rank_count'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(\n    lambda row: max([row.count(rank) for rank in set(row)]), axis=1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Explanation for Dropping Columns\n\nNo columns are explicitly dropped in this step because the original features remain valuable either independently or as components of the new engineered features. Dropping columns will only be considered after further evaluation and testing of derived feature effectiveness."]###,
