re dropped in this step\n# Reason: All original columns contribute valuable information either directly or through the engineered features. The newly created features enhance this information but do not make the existing columns redundant.\nend\n\n---\n\nBy combining these three steps, the downstream model will gain better insights into suit-based hands (like Flush/Royal Flush), rank-based hands (like One Pair/Four of a Kind), and sequential hands (like Straight/Straight Flush), significantly improving prediction performance.", "python\n# Feature name\ndf['is_flush'] = (df[['V1', 'V3', 'V5', 'V7', 'V9']].nunique(axis=1) == 1).astype(int)\n\n# Feature description\n# Binary indicator (0 or 1) denoting whether all five cards share the same suit.\n\n# Usefulness:\n# This feature directly encodes the condition for hands like Flush, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush, \n# which require all cards to share the same suit. By simplifying this pattern, it helps the model \n# focus on detecting other critical features independently.\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name\ndf['rank_run_length'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(\n    lambda row: max(row) - min(row) + 1 if len(set(row)) == 5 else 0, axis=1\n)\n\n# Feature description\n# Measures the difference between the maximum and minimum rank in a sorted hand, but only if all five ranks are unique.\n\n# Usefulness:\n# This feature quantifies the spread of ranks in the hand, which is critical for identifying sequences like \n# Straight, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush. A rank_run_length of 5 with unique cards indicates sequential ranks.\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name\ndf['pair_count'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(\n    lambda row: len([rank for rank in set(row) if row.tolist().count(rank) == 2]), axis=1\n)\n\n# Feature description\n# Counts the number of pairs in the hand, where a pair is defined as two cards sharing the same rank.\n\n# Usefulness:\n# This feature is critical for identifying hands like One Pair a